<zk>
	<label multiline="true">
		Steps to reproduce:
		1. Type "not-a-date" into the datebox and blur, then into the daterangebox begin input
			and blur. Compare the feedback.
		2. Press ESC to close the calendar, then click "change format".

		- Expected (step 1): BOTH raise the standard errorbox. The daterangebox says
			"Invalid range" and also keeps its red border (z-daterangebox-invalid).
		- Bug (step 1): only the datebox opens an errorbox; the daterangebox writes to the
			polite live region only — and its border stays unchanged, because the invalid
			rule painted with the error bubble's own border token, which is transparent.
		- Expected (step 2): the rejected text is still in the input and the red border is still
			on the box. The user has not corrected anything yet, so neither may disappear.
		- Bug (step 2): the repaint rebuilds both inputs from the last committed values, so
			"not-a-date" is silently discarded.

		Guards:
		- Correct the text ("2026/08/07"): border and errorbox clear, and a later repaint keeps
			the corrected value — the rejected text must not become sticky.
		- Type "2026-08-07" over the bad text but do NOT blur, then click "change format":
			nothing was committed, so the repaint has to read the input rather than restore what
			it used to hold — and re-derive the border from it under the new format.
		- Empty the input entirely: clearing is valid input, so the errorbox goes too.
		- Empty the input but do NOT blur, then click "change format": clearing is an edit
			like a correction, so the repaint has to leave the field empty and drop the
			border instead of restoring the rejected text.
		- With BOTH inputs holding bad text, fixing only one must NOT drop the feedback —
			parse-validity is per-field while the mark and the errorbox are whole-widget.
		- "push begin value" sends an authoritative value from the server: it replaces the
			rejected text and clears the border.
		- "server reject" makes the server throw WrongValueException. That has to raise the
			SAME pair as a client-side parse failure — errorbox AND red border — not the
			errorbox alone. "server clear" then has to take both away, not leave the border.
		- Click the errorbox's own X. That means "I have read it", not "the value is fine":
			the bubble has to go and the red border has to stay, from either origin —
			unparseable text or a server rejection. It was inert on the first and dropped
			the border on the second.
		- "server wrong value" pushes a message the client could never have produced by
			itself. With unparseable text ALSO in an input, a redraw has to re-raise THAT
			message, not downgrade it to the generic "Invalid range". And after "server
			reject" alone — nothing unparseable anywhere — a redraw has to keep the bubble
			at all, not just the border.
	</label>
	<!-- an errorbox opens to its input's right, so the buttons get their own line
		above to stay clickable while one is showing -->
	<div>
		<button id="btnFormat" label="change format"
			onClick='drb.setFormat("yyyy-MM-dd")'/>
		<button id="btnSetBegin" label="push begin value"
			onClick='drb.setBeginValue(new java.util.Date(126, 7, 7))'/>
		<!-- end first, then a begin AFTER it: setBeginValue rejects the reversed
			range, so the WrongValueException comes out of the component itself -->
		<button id="btnReject" label="server reject"
			onClick='drb.setEndValue(new java.util.Date(126, 7, 1)); drb.setBeginValue(new java.util.Date(126, 7, 20))'/>
		<button id="btnClear" label="server clear"
			onClick='org.zkoss.zk.ui.util.Clients.clearWrongValue(drb)'/>
		<!-- a message the client has no way of producing on its own, so a redraw
			showing the generic text instead is unambiguous -->
		<button id="btnWrongValue" label="server wrong value"
			onClick='org.zkoss.zk.ui.util.Clients.wrongValue(drb, "Outside the booking window")'/>
	</div>
	<div>
		<datebox id="db"/>
	</div>
	<div>
		<daterangebox id="drb" format="yyyy/MM/dd"/>
	</div>
</zk>
